DifferentialEquation/CH3/Wronskian-deter.content:
also just called `Wronskian
W(y_1, y_2, dots, y_n)(x) =
mat(delim: &quot;|&quot;,
   y_1 (x), y_2 (x), dots.c, y_n (x);
   y&#039;_1 (x), y&#039;_2 (x), dots.c, y&#039;_n (x);
   dots.v, dots.v, dots.down, dots.v;
   y_1^((n - 1)) (x), y_2^((n - 1)) (x), dots.c, y_n^((n - 1)) (x)
)
for example if n=2
W(y_1, y_2) = mat(delim: &quot;|&quot;, y_1, y_2; y&#039;_1, y&#039;_2)
then y_1, ... y_n are linearly independent if and only if W \neq 0.
This is important because in solving a linear system of differential equations, we need a fundamental set of linearly independent solutions. This is analogous to solving a system of linear equations, where the system has a unique solution only if the coefficient matrix is invertible—that is, its determinant is nonzero.

NumericalAnalysis/CH3/Singular-value-dec.content:
A = U Sigma V^T
where

A is m times n
U: An m times m Orthogonal matrix matrix whose columns are the left singular vectors of A.
Sigma: A diagonal m times n matrix containing the singular values of A in descending order.
V^T: The transpose of an n times n Orthogonal matrix where the columns are the right singular vectors of A.

Steps

calculate A A^T
calculate Eigenvalues of A A^T by solving d e t(A A^T - lambda I) = 0, where I is identify matrix
then Sigma is delta from those lambda
calculate V right singular vectors(should be unit vectors), for each lambda, (A^T A - lambda I) v = 0
calculate U left singular vectors(should be unit vectors), for each lambda, (A A^T - lambda I) u = 0

3x2 example

ref www.geeksforgeeks.org/singular-value-decomposition-svd/

A = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1, 2, 3;
3, 2, 1)
A A^T = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1, 2, 3;
3, 2, 1)
mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1, 3;
2, 2;
3, 1)
= mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1 + 4 + 9, 3 + 4 + 3;
3 + 4 + 3, 9 + 4 + 1)
= mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 14, 10;
10, 14)
det(A A^T - lambda I) &amp;=
mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 14 - lambda, 10;
10, 14 - lambda)\
&amp;= (14 - lambda)^2 - 10^2\
&amp;= 14^2 - 28 lambda + lambda^2 - 10^2\
&amp;= lambda^2 - 28 lambda + 96
(lambda - 24)(lambda - 4) = 0
Then we can get some lambda Eigenvalues, lambda_1 = 24 and lambda_2 = 4 and Singular value delta_1 = sqrt(24) and delta_2 = 2.
we can say
Sigma = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, delta_1, 0, 0;
0, delta_2, 0)
= mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, sqrt(24), 0, 0;
0, 2, 0)
For each of them, calculate eigenvectors (A^T A - lambda I) v = 0
where
A^T A = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 10, 8, 6;
8, 8, 8;
6, 8, 10)
(A^T A - 24 I) v_1 = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, - 14, 8, 6;
8, - 16, 8;
6, 8, - 14) v_1 = 0
RREF it
mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1, 0, - 1;
0, 1, - 1;
0, 0, 0) v_1 = 0
so x - z = 0 and y - z = 0
v_1 = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1/sqrt(3);
1/sqrt(3);
1/sqrt(3))
so do lambda_2 = 4
(A^T A - 4 I) v_2 = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 6, 8, 6;
8, 4, 8;
6, 8, 6) v_2 = 0
RREF it
mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1, 0, 1;
0, 1, 0;
0, 0, 0)
so x + z = 0 and y = 0
v_2 = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1/sqrt(2);
0;
(-1)/sqrt(2))
Then for v_3, since it must perpendicular to v_1,v_2, we can say v_1^T v_3 = 0 and v_2^T v_3 = 0 is true. Solve these two equations to get v_3.
v_3 = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1/sqrt(6);
- 2/sqrt(6);
1/sqrt(6))
Then
V = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, * v_1, * v_2, * v_3)
= mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1/sqrt(3), 1/sqrt(2), 1/sqrt(6);
1/sqrt(3), 0,(-2)/sqrt(6);
1/sqrt(3),(-1)/sqrt(2), 1/sqrt(6);)
Lastly need to find U with similar steps.
(A A^T - 24 I) u_1 = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, - 10, 10;
10, - 10) u_1 = 0
RREF it
mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, - 1, 1;
0, 0;)
so u_1 = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1/sqrt(2); 1/sqrt(2))
(A A^T - 4 I) u_2 = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 10, 10;
10, 10) u_1 = 0
RREF it
mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1, 1;
0, 0;)
so u_2 = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1/sqrt(2);(-1)/sqrt(2))
Then
U = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, * u_1, * u_2) = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1/sqrt(2), 1/sqrt(2);
1/sqrt(2),(-1)/sqrt(2))
Therefore
A = U Sigma V^T = mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1/sqrt(2), 1/sqrt(2);
1/sqrt(2),(-1)/sqrt(2))
mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, sqrt(24), 0, 0;
0, 2, 0)
mat(delim: &quot;[&quot;, 1/sqrt(3), 1/sqrt(2), 1/sqrt(6);
1/sqrt(3), 0,(-2)/sqrt(6);
1/sqrt(3),(-1)/sqrt(2), 1/sqrt(6);)^T
